## Description

I am interested in looking at how where someone is born and what kinds of people they grow up around impact their economic mobility. 
I am also interested in trends of where people move within the United States, and how this correlates with upward mobility. 

## Data Sources

### Data Source 1: County-Level (or Commuting Zone) Trends in Outcomes (1978-1992 Cohorts) by Parental Income, Race, and Gender

URL: https://opportunityinsights.org/data/?geographic_level=0&topic=0&paper_id=4687#resource-listing

Size: 3192 rows, 1000 columns

This dataset measures upward mobility by documenting the change in income and employment of parents and childrent between cohorts born in 1978 and 1992.

### Data Source 2: Migration Patterns by Race and Parental Income

URL: https://opportunityinsights.org/data/?geographic_level=0&topic=0&paper_id=0#resource-listing

Size: 1,000,000 rows, 12 columns

Contains count and fractions of people who move between each origin and destination commuting zone pair separately by race/ethnicity and parental income quintile. These data are unique at the OD x race/ethnicity x parental income level. The Origin/Desination locations are at the Comutting Zone level. 

### Data Source 3: Social Capital: Measurement and Associations with Economic Mobility

URL: https://data.humdata.org/dataset/social-capital-atlas

Size: 3090 rows, 26 columns
*Number of rows depends on level of data. There is County, ZIP code, High School, and College level data.

This data contains social capital statistics meaured by connectedness, cohesiveness, and civic engagement. It also measures the degree to which people from different SES interact with each other. 

